# part: Week 7 - the same program under three interpreters
# dir: week7/solution
# load: :m + APL.Parser APL.Eval
# load: import qualified APL.InterpPure as Pure
# load: import qualified APL.InterpSim as Sim
# load: let try_ run s = either id (show . run . eval) (parseAPL "" s)
? OneOf in the PURE interpreter: which side wins, and is it concurrent at all?
try_ Pure.runEval "(1+2) || (3+4+5+6)"
? The same program in the SIMULATED interpreter. Ties go which way?
try_ Sim.runEval "(1+2) || (3+4+5+6)"
? One side never finishes. Does the other still win?
try_ Sim.runEval "(loop p = 0 while true do p) || 1"
? BothOf, where one side fails
try_ Pure.runEval "(1/0) && 5"
try_ Sim.runEval "(1/0) && 5"
? QB section 7 Q8: a get on an empty store. An error, or blocked?
try_ Pure.runEval "get 0 && put 0 true"
try_ Sim.runEval "get 0 && put 0 true"
? QB section 7 Q2: tuples and projection. Which index is the first element?
try_ Pure.runEval "(1,2).0"
try_ Pure.runEval "(1,2).1"
? ... and out of range
try_ Pure.runEval "(1,2).2"
? A 1-element tuple: the AST allows it, the grammar does not
try_ Pure.runEval "(1).0"
? A tuple through a variable
try_ Pure.runEval "let x = (1,2) in x.0"
